=== Pet Spell Upgrades ===

Spell upgrades to pet spells improve the spell itself: lower mana cost, lower cast time, and each level provides 10% chance to not use a reagent.

Along with adjusting the spell itself, it also provides a boost to the pet summoned by the spell. Each increase in the spell's rank, increases the summoned pet's level by one. This increase maxes out once the summoned pet has the same level as the caster.

Each upgraded pet level will provide the following benefits:
*+6% Hit Points
*+10% base damage
*+5 to skill points

When a pet is summoned using a focus item, the pet summoned has higher base stats (more hit points, more damage per hit, etc). The bonuses from upgrading the spell levels are then applied on top of the focused pet's higher base stats.

At least some of these bonuses apply even if the summoned pet has reached max level.  If your +9 mage pet summons a level 50 pet when you are level 50, and you upgrade it to +10 - you will still have a level 50 pet, but the pet's health will still increase even though the summoned pet is the same level; pet damage is dependent on level, so that will not increase the same way. Other benefits (lower mana cost, cast time, and reagent usage) still apply.

How exactly the damage upgrade works alongside the new changes to pet weapon calculations needs to be tested (Oct 13th, 2026).

=== Pet Related AAs ===
* Companion's Discipline: Unlocks the /pet hold and /pet ghold commands.
* Companion's Fury (Magician Only): Grants access to the [[Frenzied Burnout I]] ability. This is a powerful pet buff that includes a large ATK bonus, overhaste, and causes the pet to flurry.
* Hobble of Spirits (Beastlord Only): Grants access to the [[Hobble of Spirit]] ability. This ability casts a permanent buff on your pet that lets it proc a 40% snare on its target.
* Pet Affinity: Allows Group Buffs, including Bard Songs, to affect your pet.
* Unbound Companion (Magician Only): Gives your pets an increased chance to land critical hits. Works on any pet you summon, not just Magician's elemental summons.
* Unbound Ferocity (Beastlord Only): Gives your pets up to 9% chance to flurry. Works on any pet you summon, not just Beastlord warders.

=== Pets and Experience ===

In order to receive full experience for a kill solo, you need to do at least 1 damage to the mob.  If your pet does all of the damage, experience will be greatly reduced. [Unknown group impact, if a group member does 1 damage if full xp is given to summoner as well as group]

=== Pet tracking ===

In older versions of Everquest, the '''/pet attack <npc name>''' command could be used to check if NPCs up in the zone. If the NPC is up, the pet would emote 'Attacking <npc name> Master.'

In Everquest Legends, pet tracking does <strong><em>not</strong></em> function. The pet attack command has a very short range.

=== Pet Appearance ===

There are a few available options for your pet's appearance. 

==== Pet Size ====

[[File:pet_size_ant.png|200px|thumb|'''Don't step on him!''']]

In Options >> System >> Accessibility, you can use the "Pet Size" slider to change the size of all pets from your point of view. The [[Shrink]] and [[Tiny Companion]] spells may be used to reduce the size of your pet from everybody's perspective. You can even combine the spell plus the accessibility option to make your pet positively ant-sized.

==== Pet Illusions ==== 

Some items in the game can be used to place an illusion on your pet. These come in two varieties: items which require "conversion" to a pet illusion item (ex: [[Guise of the Deceiver]]) and items that are natively pet illusion items (ex: [[Petamorph Wand: Murderbee]]). You can find the full list of items under [[:Category:Pet Illusion Items]].

To use a native pet illusion item:
# Place the item in your Inventory >> Storage >> Activated items
# Switch to the Inventory >> Pet tab
# Set the illusion at Inventory >> Pet >> Pet Illusion
# Resummon your pet 

To use a convertible item:
# Right-click on the item to open its details window
# Click the button next to 'Convert to <illusion item name>' (note: you will be prompted to remove any equipped exaltations)
# Follow the regular pet illusion item steps above

'''Note:''' You can convert the illusion item back to the original item via the same mechanism.

= Pet Equipment =
Finding new equipment for your pet is something that can continue throughout the lifetime of your character, as you only need to acquire an item once, and you can use it indefinitely on your pet, until an upgrade is found. Pet inventory items are not lost on logout, pet death, or class changes. They remain in the pet inventory until you remove them. If you change your classes and lose pet inventory slots they will be displayed as red and will not be equipped by your pet, so must be moved to an active slot to be used.
<br>
Charmed pets also use items in the pet inventory slots until charm breaks. 

== Equipment Restrictions ==
Pets can use any items that can be worn by their own classes, and any equipment you may wear.  For example, if you're a DRU/MAG/SHM and you summon a Water elemental (WAR/ROG), the pet can use any items that can be used by: Druids, Magicians, Shamans, Warriors, or Rogues.

[Can pets use items with Deity restrictions?? Needs testing in EQ Legends]

== Pet Inventory Slots ==
Pet inventory slots are given as follows.
Everyone starts with a base of 4, then you add the following slots for the following classes:

Magician and Beastlord: +3<br>
Necromancer: +2<br>
Enchanter, Druid, Shaman: +1<br>
<br>
Shadow Knight does not get an extra pet inventory slot.

So as an example, max would be the base 4 pet inventory slots + Mag 3, + Bst 3, + Nec 2 = 12 pet inventory slots.

== Pet Weapons ==

Changes have been made to how pets function with weapons throughout Everquest Legends. As of October 13th, 2026, how pets use weapons has changed once again. Giving pets weapons will increase their damage output, but the effectiveness of the weapon is based entirely on the weapon's <em>ratio</em>. Higher ratio weapons will always increase the pet's melee damage (though other factors, like stats, effects, procs, and accuracy should all be considered as well when equipping your pet).
 
With low level pets, who typically have poor dual wielding - you'll generally get better damage output with a high ratio two hander.  For higher level pets, dual wielding high ratio one handers will usually provide much better damage per second against individual targets. However, when fighting large numbers of enemies simultaneously, it is possible that a two handed weapon will provide more damage due to much higher damage cleave strikes on multiple targets.

=== Pet Damage Calculation ===
Pet attacks use a weapon damage and delay, much like players, when performing melee attacks. 

==== Delay ====
Pet attack delays are <em>fixed</em>. So a level 50 water pet will always attack with a delay of 28 (2.8 seconds) no matter what kind of weapon it has. In the early months of EQ Legends, different pets had different delays and so far it appears that the pets' base delay and damage values are unchanged. But testing needs to be done to reconfirm, and fill out the damage and delay values in the class specific pet lists below.

==== Damage ====

Pet Weapon damage is calculated by (Base Pet Damage * Mote Modifier) + (Equipped Weapon Ratio * Weapon Type Modifier).  Additionally, pets have bonus damage that is applied after the random roll to determine how much they hit the target for, just like players. Bonus damage cannot be modified, though it varies between different pets.

*''Base Pet Damage:'' This is the damage of the pet's fist, if it is using no weapon at all. This is determined by the pet you summon.
*''Mote Modifier:'' This is a damage increase from upgrading a pet spell with motes. The modifier is equal to 1 + (Spell Rank / 10).  So with a Rank 0 spell, the modifier is 1, and the pet does base damage.  At Rank X, the mote modifier increases to 2, doubling the pet's base damage.
*''Equipped Weapon Ratio:'' Fairly self explanatory - this is the ratio of the weapon your pet has equipped. Upgrading the weapon with motes increases the ratio which increases the extra damage the pet deals from having a weapon.
*''Weapon Type Modifier:'' This value is multiplied by the equipped weapon's ratio. The value is fixed regardless of pet level, though there do seem to be damage caps on low level pets' max hit.
** One-Handed Weapon Modifier: 28
** Two-Handed Weapon Modifier: 42.5

=== Dual Wielding ===

An update on July 7th, 2026 made changes to some, maybe all, pets' ability to dual wield. They now start dual wielding much earlier, and their dual wield skill improves more quickly than it used to.

As players determine which level pets actually start dual wielding, they can be updated here. This is the ''pet level'' when they begin to use both weapons, not character level.

*Beastlord Warders: 5
*Enchanter Pet: ?
*Magician Air Pet: 8
*Magician Earth Pet: 9
*Magician Fire Pet: ?
*Magician Water Pet:6
*Necromancer Pet:8
*Shadow Knight Pet: ?
*Shaman Pet: ?

'''Note:''' Pets attempt to equip weapons in the order you place them in the inventory. If you have two one-handed weapons, and one of them is Primary only and the other is Primary/Secondary only you need to equip them in the correct order to get your pet to dual wield. If you equip the Primary/Secondary weapon first, the pet will put it in their primary slot. When you then give them the Primary only weapon, they will refuse to use it. You must give them the Primary only weapon first, and then the Secondary weapon.

==== Dual Wield Measurements ====
Dual Wield measurements on the class pet charts below represent how many additional off hand attacks are made relative to main hand attacks using identical delay. A dual wield of 100% would mean the pet makes an equal number of attacks with both hands - including double and triple attacks for each hand.

=== Weapon Procs ===
Pets will use the procs on most weapons, so long as they meet the minimum required level to do so. However, they are blocked from proccing AoE effect procs, such as the [[Earthquake]] proc on [[Earthshaker]] or [[Hierophant`s Crook]].

=== Backstab Damage ===
For [[Magician]] Water Pets, who are Rogues, their maximum backstab damage is 8 * pet's backstab damage. A pet's base backstab damage is equal to their base weapon damage. If you give a pet a weapon with backstab damage, it will use the weapon's backstab damage, but only if that weapon's backstab damage is greater than their own base backstab damage.  However, since the highest backstab weapon in the current game is [[Serpent's Tooth]], with 13, and pets surpass an unarmed weapon damage of 13 at level 22 - there really isn't much reason to worry about the backstab damage of weapons you give your pets.

NOTE: It's possible that the recent pet overhaul, combined with new weapon drops in Nektropos may have changed this information somewhat. More testing needs to be done to understand how weapons work with backstab under the new system.

=== Recommended Pet Weapons ===
When starting out, just give your pets the best ratio weapons you can find that you don't need for yourself. You will usually want to use two handers, since their dual wield chance is low. By the time your pets are in their 20-30s, they are dual wielding quite frequently, so two one handers with good ratios becomes more viable.

