==== Reagents ====
Magician pets all require a [[Malachite]] to summon.

=== The Elements ===
As long as EQ has been around, the pros and cons of the mage pets has been a constant source of discussion. People have their individual play styles and the pets allow for a bit of customization depending on what you're looking for.

==== Air Elementals ====
Air Elementals suffer from being the middle of the road pets in most situations. They can be decent tanks, but not as good as Earth Elementals; and they can do decent damage, but not as good as Water Elementals. Like Water Elementals, they have an additional attack to increase their melee damage output. Unlike a Water Elemental's backstab, these monk-based strike attacks can be used from any position, keeping their damage output consistent regardless of what direction they attack from (though anyone attacking a monster from the front will have to deal with defensive skills like Riposte reducing their overall damage). Their greatest strength, however, is fighting casters. They use an instant cast, 3 second stun every 12 seconds. Combined with their normal attacks and monk strikes, this makes them quite adept at interrupting spell casting. The stun can also function like a weak root spell, helping to keep their target in place and focused on the elemental instead of yourself.

===== Pros =====
*Highest Armor Class (both Mitigation and Evasion) of all Elementals
*Extra damage from monk attacks that aren't dependent on positioning
*Casts a 3 second stun every 12 seconds, but mobs at level 56 and up are immune to stun (at least currently)
*Has a relatively high cold resistance (needs confirmation in EQ Legends)

===== Cons =====
*Second lowest hit points in the Classic Era
**In Traditional Everquest, Air Elementals start to have the second most hit points after level 50 - but there's no way to be sure if this will hold true in Legends
*Not as good at damage as Water Elementals, or as good at tanking as Earth Elementals

==== Earth Elementals ====
Earth Elementals are kings of tanking. They have the highest hit points, and cast an instant cast root spell every 16 seconds. This can keep enemies glued to your pet while you keep at range. However, they have the lowest damage output of all of the elementals in most cases - so if you already do most of the tanking, they usually aren't the best choice. However, their increased hit points may be beneficial when dealing with enemies such as [[Lord Nagafen]] who have strong AoE damage effects. 

===== Pros =====
*Has the most hit points of any of the elementals for its level
*Casts a root spell
*Has a relatively high disease resistance (needs confirmation in EQ Legends)
*Casts Ranger spells, including buffing and healing itself, and low damage AoE nukes

===== Cons =====
*Lowest damage output of all pets
*Lowest Armor Class - both mitigation and evasion - of all pets. However, the difference is minor and becomes even less significant when fully equipped and buffed.
*Spends a lot of time casting low damage Ranger nukes, as well as Ranger root and snare spells - despite already having an instant cast root ability. On the upside the snare, root, and DoTs can help it maintain agro if you need it tanking.

==== Fire Elementals ====
The "glass cannons" of the Elementals. Fire Elementals have low hit points, but they cast Wizard nukes and spells. This includes a 5 second stun (though they don't use it consistently like an Air Elemental) and roots. They can also use Wizard AoE spells - both damage spells and snares. When upgraded to level 49, and Rank VIII [[Greater Conjuration: Fire Summon]] will even cast [[Ice Comet]]. These are probably the least used of the magician pets, but are far better in Everquest Legends than they were in traditional Everquest (at least during the Classic through Planes of Power eras). When clearing large numbers of blue and light blue mobs, the Fire Elemental's nukes can allow it to help you burn through low hit point enemies very quickly.

===== Pros =====
*High burst damage
*Casts an instant cast, zero mana fire DD every 6 seconds
*Casts a self-only damage shield that is far stronger than what you can cast for a given level 
*Has a relatively high fire resistance (needs confirmed in EQ Legends)

===== Cons =====
*Lower damage in long fights once it runs out of mana
*Its spells cannot be [[Spell Upgrade System|upgraded]], which means it cannot benefit from the resist reduction that Wizards can get - so its nukes can get resisted easily against powerful targets
*Its spell list cannot be customized for a given fight
**It will use Cold Resist spells against ice creatures, Fire Resist spells against fire creatures, and cast stuns and roots on enemies that are immune to such effects
*Dies easily due to significantly lower hit points

==== Manifestation of the Elements ====
This is the pet summoned by the [[Orb of Mastery]], the reward for the [[Magician Epic Quest]]. It is not currently available in Everquest Legends, so this section mostly exists to retain information from traditional Everquest until Epic Quests have been added to Everquest Legends.

This elemental looks like a huge Air Elemental, but it is essentially a combination of all of the elementals upgraded and wrapped into one.
*Has by far the highest hit points of any summoned pet in the game
*Hits very hard and quite fast
*Casts a 50 point damage shield
*Casts a 143 DD spell (cold-based)
*Casts an 84 DD stun
*[once EQL is in Kunark, needs to be verified] Magician Epic Pets will no longer enrage at low health. Minor changes to runspeed [idem, needs to be verified, on EQ live, they run faster than regular pets but Velocity, the Kunark pet only runspeed buff, is still better] and magic resistance.

==== Water Elementals ====
Water elementals provide high sustained damage. If positioned behind their target they can backstab, and they have an instant cast direct damage spell that they cast every 6 seconds. They can also tank for you if need be, but it will lower their damage output. For most builds, water elementals are the most frequently used of all the elementals.

===== Pros =====
*2nd highest amount of hit points among elementals of the same level, at least up until level 50
**In Traditional Everquest, Air Elementals start to have the second most hit points after level 50 - but there's no way to be sure if this will hold true in Legends
*Can tank if need be, but not as well as an Earth Elemental
*Has a high poison resistance (needs confirmation in EQ Legends)
*Instant cast, cold resist, nuke every six seconds to boost damage output
*Backstabs when behind its target, making it the highest damage pet over long fights

===== Cons =====
*Needs to be positioned properly to use Backstab - will still use Backstab from the front, but it only counts as an extra normal attack dealing 25% as much damage as a backstab
*No additional utility - this pet is only good for dealing melee damage

=== Monster Summons ===
Monster Summons are pretty much just Fire Elementals, but without the Damage Shield or Wizard spells. They lack any benefit over using Elementals at all. The spell is a fun little party trick that allows mages to run around with a pet that looks like a monster from the zone they are in.

Generally monster summoning has a chance to give you a pet model from any non-playable race mob in the zone.  In Sol A, for example, you can summon a clockwork pet, but you can't summon a gnome.  Some models are extremely rare and may take many attempts to successfully "capture" them.
There are also some easter egg type models that are quite unexpected.  Dagnor's Cauldron has a [[Faydedar]] model buried in the zone file.   Other expected models, such as [[Quillmane]] in South Karana or [[Terrorantula]] in South Ro, don't seem to be summonable or are insanely rare (over 1,200 attempts).

The summoned monster pets are largely a novelty and help take the boredom away when farming in lower level zones.  Who doesn't love to watch a snow bunny slaughter the Eastern Wastes giant fort? For serious adventuring, you will want to stick with your situational elemental pets.

=== Pet Focus Items ===

''Are you looking for [[Focus Effects]]?''

(This information was provided by the Devs during a livestream)

Pet focus items a bit strange. They do not improve the stats of the pets you summon. Instead the game has tons of predefined pets - including versions of every pet summoned with a focus item that works on their respective summoning spell. So rather than the focus item just giving pets a stat buff, using the focus causes your spell to summon a <em>different</em> pet with different stats. Because the benefits are not a flat buff, the exact benefits can vary slightly from pet to pet. Information below is intended to give a rough idea of what the foci do, but are not exact.

However, the exact interaction between the pet focuses and the [[Spell Upgrade System]] are still being tested and will be updated here later.

==== Lesser Elemental Foci ====

The lesser focus items ({{:Torch of Alna}}, {{:Shovel of Ponz}}, {{:Broom of Trilon}}, and {{:Stein of Ulissa}}) are quested from the Temple of Solusek Ro, and provide the spell-level 4-49 pets with increased size and stats.

===== Benefits =====

* Bigger Pet Size
* +1 level (limited like the bonus levels from [[Spell Upgrade System|spell upgrades]])
** Does not give the same benefits as a level from the spell upgrades, it only allows them to cast new spells, if that extra level happens to unlock any.
* +15 STR
** Results in +10 Attack (Offense)
* +10 AGI
** Results in +5 Armor (Evasion)
* Extra Hit Points
** This is a bit annoying with how it works but seems consistent with how these focus effects worked in original Classic EQ. Essentially, the pet summoned when using the lesser focus has hit points equal to the next highest elemental's hit points. What that means is - a Fire Elemental will have hit points that match the hit points of an Air Elemental of the same level. A focused Air Elemental will have Water Elemental hit points. And Water Elementals will have hit points equivalent to Earth Elementals. Unfortunately, the Earth Elementals are already at the top and gain no additional hit points.

==== Greater Elemental Foci ====

The greater focus items ({{:Staff of Elemental Mastery: Water}}, {{:Staff of Elemental Mastery: Fire}}, {{:Staff of Elemental Mastery: Earth}}, {{:Staff of Elemental Mastery: Air}}) are obtained from high-end mobs and quests. These staves provide improved stats and hit points for all pets that can be summoned with them. Currently this is limited to the Greater Conjuration line of pets, but is expected to include Kunark level pets as well.

===== Benefits =====

This area needs testing, but initial results show it being much better than the Lesser Elemental Foci. However, using the focus items may be pointless once your summoning spell is upgraded to +8. Further testing needs to be done.

